INTRODUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 2a — Scientific Rationale:
REPORTED: Contextualized within post-conflict Syrian health system resilience, domestic 
manufacturing capacity (>80% local medicine needs demand), pharmacy graduate density, 
WHO Workforce 2030 global guidelines, and FIP Development Goals (Goal 4 & Goal 20).

Item 2b — Specific Objectives & Hypotheses:
REPORTED: Explicitly states study objectives investigating attitudinal-implementation gaps, 
institutional readiness constraints, and professional sector practice variations.

METHODS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 3a — Study Design:
REPORTED: Cross-sectional, observational, quantitative policy-oriented survey design.

Item 3b — Setting & Dates:
REPORTED: Conducted electronically in Syria between 10 March and 15 April 2026 via structured 
digital data collection across institutional networks, syndicate channels, and manufacturing sites.

Item 3c — Eligibility Criteria:
REPORTED: Licensed pharmacy degree holders or active undergraduate pharmacy students residing 
in Syria; exclusion criteria applied to non-pharmacy backgrounds and incomplete forms.

Item 4 — Variables & Operationalization:
REPORTED: Formative Composite Indices (PILS, IAI, IRS, IPI, and derived Diagnostic Gap) 
explicitly defined with item counts, normalization (0–100 scale), and exact floor effect metrics.

Item 5 — Measurement & Data Sources:
REPORTED: Secure electronic survey platform with automated single-response technical enforcement. 
Formative measurement philosophy explicitly justified via empirical validation literature (Coltman et al., 2008).

Item 6a — Bias Control:
REPORTED: Technical single-response restriction via IP/browser tracking; potential selection bias from 
non-probability convenience sampling is transparently disclosed as a structural limitation.

Item 6b — Study Size:
REPORTED: Target framework of 400 distributed electronic invitations; 303 verified complete 
responses successfully captured (75.75% response rate).

Item 7 — Quantitative Variables:
REPORTED: Descriptive scores managed as continuous standardized variables (0–100); raw distributions 
reported with Mean, SD, Median, IQR, Min, Max, Skewness, and Zero-inflation properties.

Item 8 — Statistical Methods:
REPORTED: Non-parametric Spearman rank correlation matrix, Kruskal-Wallis H-test for multi-group comparison, 
One-way ANOVA sensitivity analysis, Stage-1 Binary Logistic Regression, and Stage-2 Binomial GLM.

RESULTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 9 — Participant Flow & Sample Demographics:
REPORTED: Final audited case analysis sample (N = 303): 224 Undergraduate Students (73.93%), 
54 Academic Researchers (17.82%), 25 Licensed Practicing Pharmacists (8.25%); 215 Females (70.96%), 
88 Males (29.04%).

Item 10 — Descriptive Data:
REPORTED: Standardized composite metrics: PILS = 38.87 ± 15.55%, IAI = 71.30 ± 8.27%, 
IRS = 17.82 ± 20.35% (48.51% absolute zeros), IPI = 10.45 ± 17.91% (71.95% absolute zeros), 
Diagnostic Gap = 60.84 ± 19.93 points.

Item 11a/b — Associations & Multivariable Analysis:
REPORTED: Bivariate correlation matrix (Table 3); Multivariable Binary Logistic Regression (IPI > 0) 
and Binomial GLM count models (Table 4) presenting exact Odds Ratios, 95% Confidence Intervals, and p-values.

DISCUSSION & OTHER INFORMATION
